security=[
('Who holds the keys','<p>Your phone creates and uses the vault’s keys. Alvento does not receive your vault contents, passphrase, recovery key or decryption keys. There is no account reset or provider-held copy of your vault.</p><p>“Your keys” here describes key custody, not a claim to hide every trace of a file. While unlocked, Android and the app process readable data.</p>'),
('How encryption works','<p>Argon2id derives a wrapping key from your passphrase, using a random salt and 64 MiB of memory. It protects a random 256-bit vault master key. Each item has a separate random key wrapped by that master key.</p><p>Contents use Tink’s AES-GCM-HKDF streaming encryption with AES-256-GCM. The index and key wrappers use authenticated AES-GCM; thumbnails are encrypted using keys derived from item keys. Authenticated encryption detects tampering and truncation.</p><p>Changing a passphrase rewraps the same vault key. It does not change old backups or stop someone with an old secret and old vault files deriving that key from those copies.</p>'),
('On-device storage and network access','<p>The public 0.5.3 app declares no INTERNET permission, has no account or ads, and uses no developer server. It declares Google Play’s billing permission so you can buy the optional unlock, and Google Play handles the payment. Android cloud backup and device-transfer backup are disabled for its data.</p><p>A provider you choose in Android’s document picker can upload a backup under its own terms. A readable export is outside the vault’s protection. Opening a website uses your browser’s network access.</p><p>Buying or restoring the unlock needs Google Play and an internet connection. Your vault itself works offline.</p>'),
('Unlock methods','<p>Your passphrase and recovery key remain the root secrets. Optional biometric unlock uses Android’s strong-biometric authentication and a Keystore-wrapped key. Phone screen-lock and in-app pattern unlock are also available on supported devices; release builds require hardware protection for those methods.</p><p>Anyone who knows your phone’s PIN, pattern or password can open NullKey if phone screen-lock unlock is enabled. An in-app pattern is weaker than a passphrase or biometric authentication. Five wrong patterns disable that method; your passphrase still works.</p><p>Screen-lock and pattern unlock pause after an app update or seven days without typing the passphrase. Restoring a backup turns all optional unlock methods off until you enable them again.</p>'),
('Recovery and backups','<p>A recovery key opens existing encrypted data. A backup preserves a separate copy. After losing a phone, you need both the backup and a passphrase or recovery key valid for it.</p><p>Backup verification rereads the archive, authenticates the index, every encrypted item segment and every thumbnail, and matches the current vault snapshot. Restoring also authenticates the contents before installing them. A restore requires an empty vault and does not overwrite an existing one.</p><p>Practise with sample data and check restored contents. Keep the secret for each backup separately: changing today’s credentials does not update older backups. Alvento cannot recreate missing files or recover lost secrets.</p>'),
('Originals and device limits','<p>Imports keep originals by default. Optional original removal verifies eligible local copies first. Supported photos and videos require Android’s confirmation to move to trash; supported documents can be deleted permanently. Cloud and other-app copies can remain, and sync can propagate deletions.</p><p>A compromised phone, keyboard or accessibility service can expose information you type or display. File counts, approximate sizes, thumbnail presence and filesystem times are not hidden. Replacing files with an older copy of the same vault is not detected.</p><p>Release builds block ordinary screenshots; debug builds permit captures for interface review. Deleting files is not guaranteed secure erasure. Readable exports, clipboard history and other copies need separate attention.</p>'),
('What has been checked','<p>The public listing is for NullKey 0.5.3. The release evidence for the earlier 0.4.2 build records 124 JVM tests and a physical Android phone check for the updated icon and back-exit behaviour. These checks are not proof of compatibility with every phone.</p><p>This page describes documented app behaviour; it does not claim that every device was tested.</p><p><a href="'+PRIVACY+'">Read the app privacy policy</a>.</p>')]
page('/security/','Security, recovery & device limits','How NullKey encrypts your files, protects keys and checks backups, with clear limits for optional unlock methods and compromised devices.',article('Your keys.<br>Clear boundaries.','Understand what the current app protects, what it leaves on your device and what recovery depends on.',security)+simple_play_cta())

feature_pages=[
 ('private-photo-vault','A private photo vault for Android','Import private photos into an on-device encrypted vault, keep the original until you have checked the copy, and make a backup you can restore.',[
  ('Bring photos into the vault','Use Android’s document picker to select photo copies for NullKey. Open the unlocked vault and inspect a few images at full size. Originals remain in place by default.'),
  ('Know where protection applies','NullKey encrypts contents stored in its vault. A photo outside the vault, visible while the phone is unlocked, or copied to another app is outside that protection. Hidden and locked folders on Android manufacturers’ devices have their own behaviour.'),
  ('Keep a recoverable copy','Create an encrypted whole-vault backup and wait for its automatic read-back check. Store it separately from the phone and keep its matching passphrase or recovery key somewhere separate. Test restore with sample data into an empty vault.')]),
 ('encrypted-backup','Encrypted backup and restore','Make a verified encrypted copy of a NullKey vault, keep its matching secret separately and practise restoring without risking the original.',[
  ('Create the backup','In NullKey, open the backup action and choose a destination in Android’s document picker. The chosen provider controls where that file is stored and may sync it under its own terms.'),
  ('Check before relying on it','After saving, NullKey automatically rereads the archive, authenticates the index, encrypted item segments and thumbnails, and checks that it matches the vault snapshot. Wait for the message that says the backup was saved and checked; if the check fails, do not rely on that file.'),
  ('Practise a safe restore','Keep the backup and its matching passphrase or recovery key separate. Restore into an empty test vault; restore does not overwrite an existing vault. Check sample contents before treating the process as familiar.'),
  ('Remember older exports','A backup reflects the vault at export time. Later passphrase changes or recovery-key rotation do not update previous backup files. Keep the credential needed for each backup you retain.')])]
